1. PURPOSE
The Multi-Tenant Organization & Project Isolation Architecture establishes the formal tenancy model governing organizations, users, projects, resources, data boundaries and service access throughout OneDraft.
The purpose of this subsystem is to ensure that every organizational resource operates within an explicit and enforceable tenant boundary.
The architecture shall prevent unauthorized cross-organization access while allowing authorized users to collaborate within their assigned organization and projects.
Tenant isolation shall apply consistently across application services, databases, object storage, queues, workers, events, APIs, AI context, artifacts, observability and administrative operations.
2. ARCHITECTURAL ROLE
The Multi-Tenant Organization & Project Isolation subsystem forms the organizational isolation boundary of OneDraft.
It connects identity, authorization, billing, project lifecycle, data persistence, artifact management, runtime execution and API access into a consistent tenant model.
Primary responsibility: Establish and enforce organization and project isolation.
Primary inputs: Organization identity, user identity, memberships, project associations, authorization context and resource identifiers.
Primary outputs: Tenant context, project context, isolation decisions, resource access boundaries and tenant-scoped execution.
Authority: Organization and project ownership relationships shall be authoritative within the OneDraft domain model.
Security principle: Tenant isolation shall be enforced server-side and shall not depend upon frontend behavior.
3. TENANCY MODEL
OneDraft shall use a hierarchical tenancy model.
PLATFORM
   ↓
ORGANIZATION
   ↓
PROJECT
   ↓
PROJECT RESOURCES
   ↓
ARTIFACTS / JOBS / MODELS / DRAWINGS / DATA
An organization represents the principal tenant boundary.
Projects represent controlled workspaces belonging to an organization.
Resources created within a project shall inherit the applicable organization and project context unless explicitly defined otherwise.
4. ORGANIZATION
An Organization shall represent the primary tenant entity within OneDraft.
An organization may contain:
Users
Projects
Subscriptions
Entitlements
Usage Records
Billing Records
Administrative Configuration
Artifacts
API Credentials
Integration Configuration
The organization shall provide the primary ownership boundary for these resources.
5. ORGANIZATION IDENTITY
Every organization shall possess a unique immutable identifier.
The identifier shall be used throughout the application architecture to associate resources with their tenant.
Organization identifiers shall not be reused after organization deletion or archival where such reuse could create ambiguity in historical records.
6. ORGANIZATION PROFILE
Organization metadata may include:
Organization Identifier
Organization Name
Organization Type
Status
Created Timestamp
Administrative Configuration
Subscription Reference
Billing Reference
Default Settings
Organization profile information shall remain separate from individual user identity information.
7. ORGANIZATION STATES
The canonical organization states shall include:
ACTIVE
SUSPENDED
ARCHIVED
DELETED
The applicable state shall determine whether organizational operations may proceed.
Suspension or archival shall not automatically delete organizational project data.
8. ORGANIZATION MEMBERSHIP
Users shall access organization resources through explicit membership.
Membership shall identify:
User
Organization
Role
Status
Granted Permissions
Created Timestamp
Membership Source
A user shall not gain access to an organization merely by knowing an organization or project identifier.
9. MEMBERSHIP STATES
Membership may exist in states including:
INVITED
ACTIVE
SUSPENDED
REMOVED
Membership state shall be evaluated before organization resources are accessed.
Removed membership shall terminate ordinary organization access while preserving historical records required for auditability.
10. ORGANIZATION ROLES
Organizations shall support controlled roles.
Representative roles include:
Organization Owner
Organization Administrator
Project Administrator
Project Member
Reviewer
Read-Only User
Billing Administrator
The exact role model shall remain governed by the Identity, Access & Authorization architecture.
11. PROJECT OWNERSHIP
Every project shall belong to exactly one organization.
The Project entity shall therefore contain an authoritative organization reference.
Organization
    │
    ├── Project A
    ├── Project B
    └── Project C
Project ownership shall not be inferred from frontend navigation or user-selected context.
12. PROJECT ISOLATION
Projects shall provide an additional logical isolation boundary within an organization.
A user may have access to one project while being denied access to another project within the same organization.
Project authorization shall therefore be evaluated independently from organization membership.
13. PROJECT MEMBERSHIP
Projects may have explicit project memberships where project-specific access is required.
Project membership shall identify:
Project
User
Role
Permissions
Status
Granted By
Timestamp
Project membership shall be subordinate to organization membership.
14. TENANT CONTEXT
Every authenticated request that accesses tenant resources shall establish a tenant context.
The tenant context shall include, as applicable:
Organization Identifier
User Identifier
Project Identifier
Membership
Role
Permissions
Subscription State
Entitlements
The context shall be established by trusted backend services.
15. CONTEXT PROPAGATION
Tenant context shall propagate through all applicable application layers.
API
 ↓
AUTHENTICATION
 ↓
TENANT CONTEXT
 ↓
AUTHORIZATION
 ↓
APPLICATION SERVICE
 ↓
DOMAIN SERVICE
 ↓
REPOSITORY
 ↓
DATABASE
Background workers and asynchronous jobs shall receive equivalent tenant context through trusted job metadata.
16. DATABASE ISOLATION
The PostgreSQL persistence architecture shall enforce organization and project scoping.
Tenant-aware entities shall include the applicable organization identifier.
Project-owned entities shall include or deterministically derive their project context.
Repository queries shall require tenant-aware filtering.
The system shall not depend solely upon application developers remembering to add tenant predicates to every query.
17. DATABASE CONSTRAINTS
Where appropriate, database constraints shall reinforce tenant relationships.
Examples include:
Project.organization_id → Organization.id
ProjectMember.project_id → Project.id
ProjectResource.project_id → Project.id
Composite uniqueness constraints shall include organization or project scope where required.
Tenant relationships shall be validated at persistence boundaries.
18. ROW-LEVEL ISOLATION
Where appropriate, PostgreSQL row-level security or equivalent database enforcement may be used to provide an additional tenant-isolation layer.
Database-level isolation shall complement rather than replace application-level authorization.
The architecture shall maintain defense in depth.
19. REPOSITORY ISOLATION
Repositories shall provide tenant-aware data access.
A repository operation shall execute within an explicit tenant context.
The repository layer shall prevent accidental unrestricted access to tenant-owned resources.
Administrative cross-tenant operations shall require explicit privileged execution paths.
20. OBJECT STORAGE ISOLATION
Object storage shall use tenant-aware namespaces.
A logical structure may follow:
organizations/
    {organization_id}/
        projects/
            {project_id}/
                artifacts/
                imports/
                exports/
                drawings/
                models/
Object identifiers shall not expose authorization authority.
Access to an object shall require an authorization decision independent of possession of the storage key.
21. ARTIFACT ISOLATION
Generated and imported artifacts shall remain associated with their organization and project.
Artifact metadata shall contain sufficient ownership information to establish the correct tenant context.
Artifact retrieval shall verify authorization before providing access.
22. JOB ISOLATION
Asynchronous jobs shall contain tenant and project context.
A job record shall identify:
Organization
Project, where applicable.
Initiating User
Job Type
Authorization Context
Input References
Output References
Workers shall validate tenant context before processing the job.
23. WORKER ISOLATION
Workers shall not process tenant resources solely from untrusted identifiers.
A worker shall retrieve the authoritative job record and verify its organization and project context before executing the operation.
Worker execution shall preserve tenant context when accessing databases, artifacts, model data and external integrations.
24. EVENT ISOLATION
Internal events shall include tenant context where the event concerns tenant-owned resources.
An event may contain:
Organization Identifier
Project Identifier
Resource Identifier
Event Type
Event Version
Actor
Correlation Identifier
Consumers shall validate the applicable tenant context before processing tenant resources.
25. EVENT BUS SECURITY
The event bus shall prevent unauthorized consumers from receiving or processing tenant-sensitive events.
Topic or routing structures may use organization or resource scopes where appropriate.
Administrative system events shall remain distinguishable from ordinary tenant events.
26. API TENANT BOUNDARIES
API requests shall be evaluated against tenant context.
The API shall not treat a resource identifier as sufficient proof of access.
For example:
GET /projects/{project_id}
shall resolve the project and verify that the requesting principal is authorized to access the project within its organization.
27. RESOURCE AUTHORIZATION
Every tenant-owned resource shall have an identifiable ownership path.
A resource shall be resolvable through:
Resource
   ↓
Project
   ↓
Organization
or, for organization-level resources:
Resource
   ↓
Organization
Authorization shall evaluate this ownership chain.
28. CROSS-TENANT ACCESS PREVENTION
Cross-tenant access shall be explicitly denied by default.
A user associated with Organization A shall not be able to retrieve or modify resources belonging to Organization B.
This restriction shall apply even when:
Resource IDs are known
URLs are known
API requests are manually constructed
Frontend controls are bypassed
Cached identifiers are retained
29. ADMINISTRATIVE ACCESS
Privileged platform administrators may require controlled cross-tenant capabilities for operational purposes.
Such access shall use explicit administrative authorization.
Administrative access shall be:
Authorized
Scoped
Audited
Time-Bounded where appropriate
Traceable
Administrative privilege shall not silently become ordinary organization membership.
30. SUPPORT ACCESS
If OneDraft provides support personnel access to customer environments, support access shall use a dedicated controlled mechanism.
Support access shall identify:
Support Actor
Organization
Project
Purpose
Authorization
Start Time
End Time
Support access shall produce an audit record.
31. BILLING ISOLATION
Billing information shall remain organization-scoped.
A project's billing impact may be recorded through usage references, but commercial authority shall remain associated with the organization subscription.
Users shall not access another organization's billing information.
Billing administrators shall receive only the commercial access permitted by their role.
32. ENTITLEMENT ISOLATION
Entitlements shall be evaluated within organization context.
The system shall determine feature availability using the correct organization subscription and applicable project context.
One organization's subscription shall not grant capabilities to another organization.
33. ARIA TENANT ISOLATION
Aria shall operate within explicit tenant and project context.
Aria context construction shall ensure that information from one organization cannot become context for another organization.
Project-specific Aria interactions shall be limited to the authorized project's information.
Aria shall not infer authorization merely because information exists within the platform.
34. AI CONTEXT BOUNDARY
AI context construction shall enforce:
Organization Boundary
Project Boundary
User Authorization
Data Classification
Resource Authorization
Context Scope
Only information authorized for the current interaction shall be included in the AI context.
35. AI MEMORY ISOLATION
Persistent AI memory shall be explicitly scoped.
Memory may be:
User-Scoped
Organization-Scoped
Project-Scoped
System-Scoped
The system shall not permit project or organization information to be unintentionally promoted into a broader memory scope.
36. SEARCH ISOLATION
Search operations shall be tenant-aware.
Search indexes shall maintain organization and project metadata.
Search queries shall be filtered according to the user's authorized scope before results are returned.
Search ranking shall not cause unauthorized records to become visible.
37. CACHE ISOLATION
Caches containing tenant-sensitive information shall use tenant-aware keys.
Example:
organization:{organization_id}:project:{project_id}:resource:{resource_id}
Shared caches shall never return tenant-sensitive objects solely by an unscoped resource identifier.
Cache invalidation shall preserve tenant boundaries.
38. SESSION ISOLATION
Authenticated sessions shall maintain the user's authorized organization context.
Where a user belongs to multiple organizations, the active organization shall be explicitly established.
Switching organizations shall cause tenant-scoped application state to be refreshed.
Previously selected project data shall not remain visible after an unauthorized tenant switch.
39. FRONTEND TENANT CONTEXT
The frontend shall clearly identify the active organization and project.
Organization switching shall be explicit.
Project navigation shall operate only within the selected organization.
Frontend state shall be cleared or reconciled when organization context changes.
Backend authorization remains authoritative regardless of frontend state.
40. DATA EXPORT ISOLATION
Exports shall remain tenant-scoped.
An export job shall include organization and project context.
Generated export artifacts shall inherit the applicable tenant ownership.
Cross-organization export operations shall require explicit administrative authority where supported.
41. IMPORT ISOLATION
Imported data shall be associated with the organization and project initiating the import.
External source files shall not become accessible to another organization unless explicitly transferred through an authorized mechanism.
Import processing jobs shall preserve the originating tenant context.
42. INTEGRATION ISOLATION
External integrations shall maintain tenant-specific credentials and configuration.
Integration records shall identify their owning organization.
External service calls shall use the credentials and configuration associated with the authorized tenant.
Shared platform integrations shall not expose one organization's credentials or project information to another organization.
43. OBSERVABILITY ISOLATION
Tenant-sensitive telemetry shall be appropriately scoped.
Logs, traces and metrics shall contain tenant identifiers where required for operational diagnosis while minimizing unnecessary exposure of customer data.
Users shall not gain access to another organization's operational telemetry.
Platform operators may access broader telemetry through controlled administrative permissions.
44. BACKUP ISOLATION
Backups shall preserve tenant ownership relationships.
Restore operations shall maintain organization and project associations.
A tenant-specific restore shall not accidentally overwrite or expose another organization's resources.
Disaster-recovery procedures shall be tested for tenant-boundary integrity.
45. DATA LIFECYCLE
Tenant ownership shall remain preserved throughout the data lifecycle.
Retention, archival and deletion operations shall respect organization and project boundaries.
Deleting or archiving one organization's data shall not affect another organization's resources.
Historical records shall retain sufficient ownership metadata to preserve provenance.
46. ORGANIZATION TRANSFER
OneDraft may support controlled transfer of a project between organizations where product policy permits.
A transfer shall require explicit authorization from the applicable organizations.
The operation shall establish:
Source Organization
Destination Organization
Project
Authorized Actors
Effective Time
Transfer Record
Updated Ownership
Historical ownership shall remain preserved.
47. PROJECT CLONING
Project cloning shall create a new project identity.
A clone shall not inherit unrestricted access to the source project's authorization context.
The new project shall belong to the explicitly selected destination organization.
Copied resources shall receive appropriate new ownership references.
48. TENANT-AWARE API & DATA MODEL
Representative tenant-aware entities include:
Organization
OrganizationMember
Project
ProjectMember
TenantContext
TenantResource
TenantAccessGrant
OrganizationConfiguration
ProjectOwnershipRecord
OrganizationTransfer
SupportAccessSession
TenantAuditRecord
These entities shall integrate with the existing User, Project, Subscription, Entitlement, ModelVersion, Artifact, Job, Event and Authorization structures.
49. ARCHITECTURAL INVARIANTS
The following invariants shall govern the Multi-Tenant Organization & Project Isolation Architecture.
Single Ownership: Every project shall belong to one organization at a given point in time.
Default Denial: Cross-tenant access shall be denied unless explicitly authorized.
Backend Enforcement: Tenant isolation shall not depend upon frontend behavior.
Context Integrity: Tenant context shall be preserved across synchronous and asynchronous execution.
Data Isolation: Databases, artifacts, caches, events, jobs and AI context shall preserve tenant boundaries.
Authorization: Resource identifiers shall never constitute authorization.
AI Isolation: Aria shall not receive unauthorized cross-tenant context.
Billing Isolation: Commercial information shall remain organization-scoped.
Administrative Control: Cross-tenant administrative operations shall require explicit privileged authorization.
Historical Integrity: Ownership changes shall preserve historical records.
No Silent Transfer: Projects shall not change organizational ownership without an explicit controlled operation.
Defense in Depth: Application, repository, database and infrastructure controls shall reinforce one another.
50. COMPLETION STATUS
The Multi-Tenant Organization & Project Isolation Architecture establishes the complete organizational and project isolation model for OneDraft.
The subsystem defines organization ownership, memberships, project boundaries, tenant context, database isolation, object-storage isolation, job and worker isolation, event isolation, API boundaries, billing and entitlement isolation, Aria context isolation, search and cache isolation, integration boundaries, observability, backup, data lifecycle, organization transfers and project cloning.
The architecture establishes the tenant boundary required for OneDraft to operate as a secure multi-organization platform while preserving the integrity and independence of project data and services.
The next architectural layer establishes the API Gateway, Rate Limiting & Service Protection architecture, defining how external and internal requests are admitted, authenticated, authorized, throttled, protected and routed through the OneDraft platform.
